  "history_summaries": [
    "Repair round 1: Fix assertion `interested0_true_in_critical_path` (DVF assertion error). Root cause: DUT bug in the state machine at L0→L1 transition — `interested(self)` was set only in the L1 case (line 210), but `self` can change between the L0→L1 transition cycle and the L1 evaluation cycle, leaving pc(0)=L1 with interested(0)=0 permanently. Fix: Added `interested(self) := true.B` in the L0 case (line 207) right before `pc(self) := Loc.L1`, making the interested flag atomic with the critical-path entry. The existing `interested(self) := true.B` at L1 is preserved for redundancy. All assertions/liveness properties preserved exactly (no labels changed, deleted, or weakened). No homologous assertions found — only processor-0 assertions exist, and `interested0_false_when_idle` already passes because L7 correctly clears interested in the same cycle.",
    "Repair round 2: Fix 4 remaining failing liveness properties (p0_active_interest_progress, p0_progress_L1_to_L7, p1_progress_L1_to_L7, p2_progress_L1_to_L7). Error type: dut_bug. Root cause: In the chain-based N-process Peterson algorithm, the L5 wait state only transitioned back to L4 without advancing `j(self)`. Since `j(self)` was permanently set to `self + 1` at L3, the L4 condition `j(self) === self` was never true, causing infinite L4↔L5 oscillation and preventing ANY process from ever reaching the critical section (L6) or exit (L7). Fix: In L5 (lines 237-244), when the wait condition `interested(k) && (turn === k)` clears, advance `j(self)` to the next competitor (+1 mod HIPROC+1) before transitioning to L4. This makes each process iterate through all higher-numbered competitors (self+1, self+2, ..., 7, 0, ..., self-1) and finally reach `j(self) === self` at L4 to enter L6. The chain traversal completes in at most 8 L5→L4 iterations, well within the 50-80 cycle bounds. All assertions/liveness properties preserved exactly (no labels changed, deleted, or weakened). No homologous assertions — this is a DUT state-machine bug affecting all processes uniformly via the same L5 transition.",
    "Repair round 3: Fix 6 remaining failing properties (mutex_pc0_pc1_pc2_L6, interested0_false_when_idle, p0_active_interest_progress, p0_progress_L1_to_L7, p1_progress_L1_to_L7, p2_progress_L1_to_L7). Error type: dut_bug. Root cause: The L6 critical-section-exit logic (lines 253-258) had a special-case pause trap `when(io.pause && (self === 0.U || self === 1.U || self === 2.U)) { pc(self) := Loc.L6 }` that permanently trapped processes 0,1,2 in the critical section when pause was asserted. This caused deadlock: if io_pause==1 while self==0 at L6, pc(0) stayed L6 forever, making progress to L7 impossible. Fix: Replace the conditional L6 logic with unconditional `pc(self) := Loc.L7`, allowing all processes to exit the critical section regardless of pause state. The pause mechanism at L0 (line 203) already prevents idle-entry into the protocol during pause, so the L6 trap is unnecessary — removing it lets processes correctly complete their critical section, clear interested at L7, and idle at L0 until pause is deasserted. This fix addresses the causal path (pause-induced deadlock at L6 blocking L7 exit) rather than the observed symptom (process 0 stuck at L6 while self cycles between other values). All assertion labels preserved exactly — no labels changed, deleted, or weakened. No homologous assertions — the L6 pause trap was unique to the L6 state and only one such construct exists in the source."
  ],
